#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:5
#: a217dd95ce9548b49b2663c45ceabd78
msgid "Aggressive Rate Loop Tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:7
#: de8364835fed484db5a38acb49b28183
msgid "Normally, the attitude rate PID controllers update at the :ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>`, the main loop speed and noise filtering at half the loop rate. This means that PID rate error corrections can happen only at 1/loop rate. For 400hz, this is 2.5ms. In ArduCopter 4.7 and later, it is possible to run the PID attitude rates closer to the rate at which the gyros provide measurements, which can be up to 4KHz, resulting in 2.5uS rate corrections. Tuning is more sensitive and complex, but for aerobatic applications on vehicles with large thrust to inertia ratios (ie very agile) could yield much more precise and responsive control."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:9
#: 308ab66d5699461d8e7f9224d5327d77
msgid "this is much more computationally intensive, resulting in higher CPU loads. On H7 based autopilots it is acceptable, but F4 based autopilots might not be able to sustain this increased load."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:11
#: e3c22c08d8f3481e9d80642a193ee579
msgid "To setup running the PID rate loops closer to the higher gyro sampling rates, rather than the :ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:14
#: f7ac91ee57bf4c73ac68d1d94b7c333d
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:15
#: c015c15d8bd24d83892ed44474ea2981
msgid "There are only two new settings for enabling fast rate attitude, but these have an effect on several other settings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:17
#: 092b5f80293943fa8b6db956e95072a2
msgid ":ref:`FSTRATE_ENABLE<FSTRATE_ENABLE>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:18
#: 489fb5e83f164f9380f67a987a0a52f0
msgid "0: Disables this feature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:19
#: 6df6d326b3da4a1a9b6d245e2f9ee6b7
msgid "1: Enable dynamic fast rate. On high CPU loading (as determined by either the main loop being slow or the gyro sample queue filling up) the rate will be reduced to an integer divisor of the gyro data rate until the CPU stabilizes. This is the safest way of trying the support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:20
#: 3f1c5089fbbe45b483ed63e8580df446
msgid "2: Enable dynamic fast rate, but fix to the requested rate on arming. This allows un-armed operations such as log download to proceed effectively. Note that you must be sure that you have sufficient CPU to enable this option or control can be compromised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:21
#: 0c60e7792eba4680ba278ba078defa8b
msgid "3: Enable fixed fast rate. This is useful for testing but not usually used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:23
#: 1701feaa7c12481c8d9c86137d4b9b07
msgid ":ref:`FSTRATE_DIV<FSTRATE_DIV>`: this defaults to 1 and represents the minimum fast rate gyro divisor. So a value of 2 will yield a rate loop of gyro rate / 2 (:ref:`INS_GYRO_RATE<INS_GYRO_RATE>` / 2). This allows a fixed fast rate that is lower than the gyro rate. This parameter is can be modified in-flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:25
#: 6c9a2de145b14bf39cb49a5336e3e328
msgid "The easiest way to try this feature is to set :ref:`FSTRATE_ENABLE<FSTRATE_ENABLE>` = 1 (Dynamic rate). If your flight controller is struggling it will reduce the attitude loop rate until normality is restored. :ref:`FSTRATE_ENABLE<FSTRATE_ENABLE>` = 2 (Fixed Rate) is not recommended until you have seen reasonable performance with dynamic rates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:27
#: 083a5021ce8240a5bd225ae2caef0388
msgid "One way to gain more CPU is to switch off additional IMUs using :ref:`INS_ENABLE_MASK<INS_ENABLE_MASK>` or reduce :ref:`INS_GYRO_RATE<INS_GYRO_RATE>`. The main CPU load is from the INS and attitude control threads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:29
#: 2c30cf52dbf346a4ab469a9baa7078be
msgid "When this feature is enabled, several other settings are affected:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:31
#: a7f37181c907459297528f08c5463c2f
msgid ":ref:`SERVO_DSHOT_RATE<SERVO_DSHOT_RATE>` - this represents a multiplier of the main loop rate to output to the motors. When the fast rate loop is enabled this is normally set to 1, however if the calculated rate will not exceed the gyro rate, higher values are allowed. For instance if :ref:`INS_GYRO_RATE<INS_GYRO_RATE>` = 2(4KHz) and :ref:`FSTRATE_DIV<FSTRATE_DIV>` = 4 (1KHz)then values of :ref:`SERVO_DSHOT_RATE<SERVO_DSHOT_RATE>` up to 4(4x loop rate) are allowed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:32
#: 985a30087fee416db6c0e3d493485931
msgid "Fast attitude and PID logging enabled in :ref:`LOG_BITMASK<LOG_BITMASK>` - setting these bits usually logs at the main loop rate, however if fast attitude is enabled these will be logged at the minimum of the fast attitude rate and 1kHz"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:33
#: b6c2f7559ca04fbd8a84d2a2f65a3bdd
msgid ":ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>` - since all the heavy lifting of attitude control is being done in a separate thread, :ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>` can be run at normal values - for instance 400Hz or even less."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:36
#: 095681aae56e45e39602b4bc4aacfdc0
msgid "Tuning Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:37
#: 52ae995a8cb3481a89eadded22a0a19e
msgid "Its extremely important that the :ref:`Notch filtering <common-imu-notch-filtering>` be setup as well as the :ref:`tuning-process-instructions` be followed before tuning with this feature enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:39
#: 16f3f5bda87c4133b3f11727f7bcad56
msgid ":ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>` - as high as you can get without cooking your motors. Often copters have resonance around 120Hz so it is hard to get higher than this without notching out frame resonance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:40
#: 4ca46cfa334f49c69c69ad181fc99f24
msgid "each axis' ``ATC_RAT_xxx_FLTD`` should be :ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>`/2 on roll and pitch and :ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>`/4 on yaw. This is because D is more active, lets more noise through and is more likely to cook your motors. Setting it above 0.75 * :ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>` is not recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/high-loop-rate-tuning.rst:41
#: f6b9ea8f28fd4ac181b81a6153819f26
msgid "each axis' ``ATC_RAT_xxx_FLTT`` should be at the highest rate you want the EKF or your fingers to control the copter. Values of 30Hz should be fine, but higher has diminishing returns but does not affect the tune."
msgstr ""
